WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.760
Two satellites close from 189 kilometers to 47 in 4 days at jogging pace. The United States Space Force ran that pass as a rendezvous test. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 235. NASA's Swift Observatory, reflective clouds on a lava world, and a possible first moon beyond our solar system follow. Jackal and Puma share a sun-synchronous orbit roughly 500 kilometers up, and public orbit data posted to Reddit has been tracking the pair.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:36.700
Jackel, built by true anomaly and launched on May 3rd, performed three small burns on October 22nd and 23rd.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:46.560
Puma, built by Rocket Lab, and launched on June 19th with under 17 hours notice, answered with large orbit changes in late July and at the end of August.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.660
Daily separation distances then fell to 83 kilometers, then 54, then 69.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:59.280
9. Relative speed at closest approach dropped from 11 to 14 meters per second on October 23rd

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:07.780
to 7.6 meters per second the next day, then 3.5 on October 26th and 3.4 by October 27th.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.160
The next predicted pass places the pair 2.3 kilometers apart at that reduced speed.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.660
Public tracking data updates a few times daily and carries roughly 1 kilometer uncertainty

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.366
at this altitude. NASA ended the Swift Observatory after 22 years. Live science reported,

00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:30.766
following an unsuccessful attempt by a private satellite to rendezvous with the aging spacecraft.

00:01:31.326 --> 00:01:36.326
Over its lifetime, the mission studied the universe's most powerful explosion since the big bang.

00:01:36.966 --> 00:01:43.240
That record of gamma ray burst observations is now closed. Samuel Boucher and colleagues at the

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:50.680
The University of Waterloo used JWST's near-spec instrument to watch the lava planet T-O-I-561-B

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.800
for 37 hours across nearly four full orbits, according to a report on Reddit.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.860
Phase curve measurements showed the planet reflects about three-fourths of incoming starlight,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.060
comparable to Venus, while heat is redistributed rather than confined to the dayside.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.080
The campaign captured the planet's full orbit and found that reflected starlight explains

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:17.000
the unexpectedly cool dayside. Climate simulations point to clouds of vaporized silicate minerals,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.560
such as silicon dioxide and magnesium silicate, acting as a reflective layer on the permanent

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.640
dayside. Those silicate clouds act like a planetary sunscreen, keeping temperatures low enough for

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.500
an atmosphere to persist. The findings suggest the atmosphere is replenished by gases from the planet's

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.460
global magma ocean, and the same clouds may seed the thick envelope that ocean continually replenishes.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:46.120
Boucher began the project as an undergraduate research intern at the Trachea Institute for

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.780
Research on Exoplanets, and the work is now a published study.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.820
Future observations at longer wavelengths could distinguish whether the atmosphere resembles

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:58.620
a runaway greenhouse Venus or an extreme volcanic IO.

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:04.968
Astronomers reported evidence for a roughly Jupiter mass satellite orbiting a distant exoplanet.

00:03:05.628 --> 00:03:07.348
News.amtech reported.

00:03:07.348 --> 00:03:11.548
Confirmation would mark the first satellite found beyond our solar system.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.988
The candidate sits at a mass comparable to Jupiter itself.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.448
Detection methods are reaching that regime for the first time.

00:03:19.521 --> 00:03:25.101
A gamma ray burst remained active for 27 days, far longer than typical events of its class.

00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:27.061
Space.com reported.

00:03:27.721 --> 00:03:31.501
Astronomers recorded the event as the longest burst of its kind on the books.

00:03:32.081 --> 00:03:37.181
The extreme duration offers new constraints on the physics of the most powerful cosmic explosions.

00:03:37.181 --> 00:03:42.617
versions. Perseverance collected data on dust particles clinging to spacesuit fabric samples

00:03:42.617 --> 00:03:50.057
over 1,645 Martian days NASA reported. Five swatches, each three quarters of an inch

00:03:50.057 --> 00:03:55.837
square, formed part of the Sherlock calibration target. Pixel detected fine particles under

00:03:55.837 --> 00:04:02.197
2.5 micrometers on the Teflon and Vectran samples. The same instrument found ultra-fine particles

00:04:02.197 --> 00:04:07.197
around 100 nanometers on the basaltic glass and an additional Teflon swatch.

00:04:07.657 --> 00:04:12.257
Static charge on different calibration target materials determined which particle sizes

00:04:12.257 --> 00:04:17.437
adhered most strongly, with the finest grains remaining attached through years of driving and

00:04:17.437 --> 00:04:22.237
drilling. The measurements will inform future suit and habitat dust mitigation designs,

00:04:22.817 --> 00:04:28.656
including suit ports and dual chamber air locks. Compact stellar systems in the Young Universe

00:04:28.656 --> 00:04:34.496
observed by JWST, suggest collisions between stars occurred at higher rates than today.

00:04:35.116 --> 00:04:39.856
Fizz.org reported. The dense environments allowed stars to interact more frequently

00:04:39.856 --> 00:04:44.516
before galactic disks expanded. Those early collisions would have been far more

00:04:44.516 --> 00:04:47.956
common than the stellar encounters seen in today's expanded galaxies.

00:04:48.999 --> 00:04:53.079
Parker's solar probe data show braided magnetic structures forming and growing

00:04:53.079 --> 00:04:58.099
within the solar wind. Universe today reported. These features link large-scale

00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:01.599
plasma flows to the behavior of individual ions near the sun.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:06.459
The twisted magnetic braids appear in the probe's solar wind measurements as they form

00:05:06.459 --> 00:05:06.959
and grow.

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:12.409
Earth's magnetic field stretches across a volume hundreds of times larger than the planet

00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:17.669
itself, forming an invisible bubble that reaches tens of thousands of kilometers into space.

00:05:18.269 --> 00:05:23.109
Picture a city-sized refrigerator magnet whose field lines extend from one end of a continent

00:05:23.109 --> 00:05:23.789
to the other.

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:28.769
That is the relative scale of the magnetosphere compared with the solid Earth beneath it.

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:33.549
Inside that bubble, the solar wind arrives as a steady stream of charged particles,

00:05:34.209 --> 00:05:38.249
and when those particles slam into the outer boundary, they set up waves that ripple

00:05:38.249 --> 00:05:39.909
asymmetrically around the planet.

00:05:40.589 --> 00:05:45.429
Researchers identified how incoming solar wind pressure creates those uneven wave patterns

00:05:45.429 --> 00:05:46.749
across the magnetosphere.

00:05:47.229 --> 00:05:49.189
On the day side, the field is compressed.

00:05:49.589 --> 00:05:51.829
On the night side, it stretches into a long tail.

00:05:51.829 --> 00:05:56.669
The waves that form at the boundary carry information about where the pressure is greatest

00:05:56.669 --> 00:05:58.529
and where the shield is working hardest.

00:05:59.129 --> 00:06:03.829
Those patterns reveal where the magnetic field works hardest to deflect cosmic rays and

00:06:03.829 --> 00:06:04.709
solar radiation.

00:06:05.309 --> 00:06:09.969
The field shields the atmosphere from gradual stripping by the solar wind while also handling

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:12.109
radiation from distant supernovas.

00:06:12.999 --> 00:06:17.639
Measurements show that the finest dust-sized particles in the solar wind can still leak through

00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:23.059
at certain latitudes, while larger cosmic ray nuclei are deflected far more effectively.

00:06:23.739 --> 00:06:28.419
The same asymmetry that concentrates pressure on one flank also channels some particles

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:32.479
toward the polar cusps, where they spiral down and create the aurora.

00:06:33.099 --> 00:06:36.139
Yet the exact efficiency of that filtering remains uncertain.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:41.539
We can map the waves and count the particles that reach low Earth orbit, but we still do

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:46.459
not know why the leakage rate changes from one solar wind gust to the next, or how much

00:06:46.459 --> 00:06:50.699
the shield's protective power will shift if Earth's magnetic field continues its slow

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:51.159
weakening.

00:06:52.086 --> 00:06:56.666
Construction crews tilted the nearly 2,000-ton main structure of the extremely large

00:06:56.666 --> 00:07:00.006
telescope for the first time, according to a report on Reddit.

00:07:00.626 --> 00:07:02.726
